// StudyProtocol.swift
// PrimaeNative
//
// WHICH VERSION OF THE CHILD'S EXPERIENCE A ROW WAS RECORDED UNDER
// (2026-10-04).
//
// The export carried no build identity at all: the app version is the
// constant "1.0-1" in every configuration, and the comparison stamp
// (`StudyComparisonConfiguration.nonDefaultStamp`) lists switches that
// DIFFER FROM THE CURRENT DEFAULTS — so when a default itself moves, a
// row from before the move and a row from after it both stamp nothing,
// although the children heard different things. Two such moves happened
// mid-pilot (#27 and #32, below), and one change (#27's free-writing
// sound) is not a switch at all, so no stamp could ever show it.
//
// `revision` is a hand-bumped integer, stamped onto every
// `PhaseSessionRecord` WHEN THE RECORD IS CREATED (never at export time,
// for the same reason as `comparisonConfiguration`: an export can run
// days later on a build that has moved on). Bump it, and append a row
// to `history`, in the same PR as ANY change to what a study child
// sees, hears or does. `StudyProtocolTests` pins that the history is
// contiguous from 1 and ends at `revision`.
//
// Rows written before this field existed decode as nil. They are r1–r3
// and can only be told apart by `recordedAt` against the dates below
// and the device's install log — merge time is not install time.

import Foundation

nonisolated enum StudyProtocol {

    /// One child-facing protocol change.
    nonisolated struct Revision: Sendable, Equatable {
        let revision: Int
        /// Merge to main, UTC. Nil until the revision's PR is merged.
        let mergedAtUTC: String?
        /// Pull request on the forge. Nil until the PR exists.
        let pullRequest: Int?
        let childFacingChange: String
    }

    /// The protocol revision this build records. Always the last entry
    /// of `history`.
    static let revision: Int = 17

    static let history: [Revision] = [
        Revision(revision: 1, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-01 22:33", pullRequest: 21,
                 childFacingChange: "Baseline as of the Xcode 27 pin. Study free-writing and every cold probe are sound-off; speech is off in every arm; guided sound needs the pen on the path at >= 22 pt/s."),
        Revision(revision: 2, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-03 16:12", pullRequest: 27,
                 childFacingChange: "Sound arms hear their sound while free-writing, including the outcome passes (gate removed). Spoken prompts on for the phoneme and spatial arms only; prompts reworded. End-of-set celebration and hold. Observe starts on enrolment. No ink carried into free-writing."),
        Revision(revision: 3, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-04 09:38", pullRequest: 32,
                 childFacingChange: "Guided and free-writing sound gated on the pen being on the path only: velocity floor 22 -> 0 pt/s."),
        Revision(revision: 4, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-04 21:11", pullRequest: 34,
                 childFacingChange: "Spoken content identical in all three arms, the silent arm included: the phase prompts, \"Probier's nochmal\" and the end-of-set \"Super gemacht!\" (score-dependent praise stays unreachable); the end-of-set chime and the stroke tick stay sound-arm only. The observe prompt is spoken once per observe phase and finishes before the arm's observe sound starts — prompt first, then sound, where both used to fire together; the sound arms then hear their sound, steady, for the rest of the observe animation, replacing the 2 s demonstration in observe. A cold probe is refused when the current arm's recording for that letter is missing, so no outcome pass runs silent in a sound arm."),
        // r5 also records #39 (2026-10-05), which changed what the child
        // sees and hears in observe and missed its own bump: rows written
        // between #39 and this revision carry 4 but heard the #39 observe.
        Revision(revision: 5, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-05 19:38", pullRequest: 42,
                 childFacingChange: "Observe (#39, missed bump): the animation and, in the sound arms, the sound start together after the instruction, sound for the whole pass. Observe sound now TRACKS the animated dot like the pen (rate from dot velocity, pan from x, spatial pitch from y; still dot = slowest rate), reversing D9's steady carrier. The presentation starts 1.0 s after the spoken cue ends (3.0 s fallback from the cue's start), all arms. Envelope in every writing phase of the sound arms: fade-out 0.4 s (was 0.12), stall 0.3 s (was 0.12), lift hold 0.8 s (was 0; a re-touch inside it keeps the sound)."),
        Revision(revision: 6, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-05 20:36", pullRequest: 43,
                 childFacingChange: "Free-writing (the session free-write and every cold probe) is ungated: in the sound arms the sound follows the pen anywhere on the canvas, no longer only near the reference letter's own position and size; guided keeps the on-letter gate. The free-writing score (and so the stars) and the primary outcome spatialDeviation are measured after fitting the drawn letter's bounding box onto the reference's (translate + uniform scale, aspect kept; a scribble under 2% of the canvas is moved, not scaled); the unnormalised distance is exported as spatialDeviationRaw."),
        Revision(revision: 7, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-14 18:44", pullRequest: 46,
                 childFacingChange: "Sound arms, every writing phase (guided, free-writing, cold probes): a pen put back down inside the 0.8 s lift hold now keeps the sound, as r5 specified. Under r5-r6 the touch-down reloaded the arm file, which hard-stopped the held sound, and nothing restarted it: the strokes after the first stayed silent for as long as each re-touch came inside the hold, until the pen paused 0.3 s or lifted for longer than the hold. Sound arms after an arm change in the same app run (the next child enrolled in place, or a comparison cycle): the engine follows the arm. Under r1-r6 an app launched in the silent arm kept the silent engine for every later child, so a phoneme or spatial child enrolled after it heard no arm sound at all (observe, guided, free-writing, cold probes) while the rows recorded the sound arm; now they hear it, and the silent arm never reaches the real engine. Every arm change and every new child also stops the previous sound and resets rate, pan and pitch to neutral: under r1-r6 a phoneme child after a spatial child heard every phoneme pitch-shifted by that child's last pen height (up to +-1200 cents). Silent arm unchanged for the child: still silent. Every arm, guided and free-writing (training and cold probes): a slow pen leaves ink and is recorded. The 1.5 pt jitter filter now measures from the last point it kept, not from the previous touch sample; under r1-r6 any motion slower than about 180 pt/s (finger, 120 Hz) or 360 pt/s (Pencil, 240 Hz) drew no ink and was left out of the scored, exported free-writing trace, and so was the slowing end of every stroke. The free-writing trace now also keeps the pen's last position before the lift, so its span runs from the pen-down to the last sample before the lift. A pen held still adds no points: a sample under 1.5 pt from the last kept point is not kept, so jitter around a resting finger records the pen-down alone. A contact that creeps 1.5 pt or more does add points, so a free-writing contact now counts as a production (it starts the 2 s quiet-window advance, and is scored and recorded as a completed trial when the proctor moves on inside that window) only if it has at least two points spanning at least 2% of the canvas (the larger side of its bounding box, canvas-normalised: the r6 scribble extent); under r1-r6 two recorded points were enough. A smaller contact is treated like a one-sample palm. Sound, velocity and the guided checkpoint tracker are unchanged: they always saw every sample. Cold probes (pretest, post-test, delayed), every arm: a probe of the trained set's last letter no longer ends the set; under r2-r6 it spoke \"Super gemacht!\" (all arms), chimed (sound arms) and held the chevron, so training could not be reached by the chevron. A finished probe no longer advances by itself into the next trained letter's demonstration (and, in the sound arms, its sound); the canvas stays on the finished probe until the proctor moves on. After the set ends, the left chevron and the random-letter action hold like the right chevron instead of reopening a trained letter. A chevron (either) or the random-letter action tapped inside the 2 s quiet window after the set's last letter now closes the finished free-write first, so it ends the set and stays on that letter; under r2-r6 the set ended (phrase, chime, hold) and the same tap then opened another trained letter, the first one for the right chevron, starting a second pass under the same participant. Comparison runs only (letter-repeat switch above 1): a finished cold probe is no longer repeated as a demonstration and training pass of that letter."),
        Revision(revision: 8, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-14 18:46", pullRequest: 47,
                 childFacingChange: "What the child and the data get from batches B, C and D. DATA: a NaN phase score is stored as NaN, earns no star, and no store or aggregate (average, trend, scheduler priority, export) turns it into a number any more; under r1-r7 it could surface as a score or a star. The participant identity, its arm assignment and its archive seal are restored and written durably (a launch after a crash or a kill keeps the same child and arm). INTERRUPTIONS, every arm: .inactive (a notification banner, Control Center, an edge swipe, the app switcher) suspends the session instead of finalising it, and the session resumes on return; an observe phase that was interrupted restarts from the instruction cue in every arm (D9 default), so the sound and the animation are never heard from the middle. Opening the parent area suspends the session the same way. In an enrolled study session the screen is kept awake (no auto-lock mid-trial); the system edge gestures are deferred always, enrolled or not (a first swipe from the edge is absorbed, not acted on). SPEECH, every arm: the study phase instruction waits out the fade of the previous sound before it starts (R-029), so the cue is no longer spoken over a fading sound or clipped by it. A spoken line that is still in flight is finished before the next one starts (hand-over, with a watchdog that yields to an end report the system already made, and releases a line the system never reports after its budget of 4 s + 0.25 s per character); stop() halts the line in flight and drops every waiting line. The German voice is pinned on first use and stamped on every row (voiceId); a session that would speak is refused when no German voice can be used or the pinned voice is gone, and the proctor can clear and re-pin the voice from the parent area without an uninstall (R-021). PROCTOR: a banner warns when another app is playing audio (otherAudioPlaying, secondaryAudioHint; R-018); a comparison run (letter-repeat switch above 1) is named at enrolment, on the proctor card and in the export (R-017); the recognizer model is warmed at launch and a failed load is retried once, with its availability recorded per row (recognizerAvailable; R-034); probe attempts are numbered (probeAttempt) and starting a repeat of a cold probe, or a post-test before the child's set is complete, asks the proctor to confirm (warned, never blocked; R-032). EXPORT, phase rows, appended after spatialDeviationRaw, newest-last: participantId, rowId, finalizedBy, probeAttempt, observeRestarts, strokesCancelled, routeChanges, interruptions, outputRouteAtStart, outputRouteAtEnd, outputVolume, outputLatency, otherAudioPlaying, secondaryAudioHint, voiceId, recognizerAvailable, guidedAccess, idleTimerDisabled, lowPowerMode, thermalState, batteryLevel, launchId, previousRunAbnormal, appVersion, buildConfiguration, buildUUID, osVersion, deviceModel. Duration rows now carry participantId, rowId, trainedSubset, comparisonConfiguration and protocolRevision; every row names its participant and a row id (R-123)."),
        Revision(revision: 9, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-14 20:24", pullRequest: 50,
                 childFacingChange: "The five phrases a study child hears are now RECORDED audio in one voice (\"Kiki\", ElevenLabs eleven_multilingual_v2), played from bundled files in every arm alike: \"Schau genau hin.\", \"Jetzt du.\", \"Und jetzt ganz allein.\", \"Probier's nochmal.\" and \"Super gemacht!\". Under r1-r8 they were spoken by the iPad's own German system voice, which depends on the device and its installed voices. The texts are those listed in MANIFEST.json (the same five phrases the system voice spoke); spoken content is still identical in all three arms (D15). OBSERVE TIMING: the animation (and, in the sound arms, the sound) still starts 1.0 s after the instruction ENDS, or 3.0 s after it started if no end is reported, whichever comes first; the end is now taken from the end of the recording instead of the speech synthesiser's end report, with the same handling of a cut or stale instruction (a cut instruction starts nothing). The observe instruction is now the recording's own length: phase_observe.mp3 is 1.25 s (afinfo on the bundled file: 1.2539 s), where the system voice took about 2.4 s, so the observe presentation starts earlier (instruction end + 1.0 s), identically in all arms. RETRY CUE: \"Probier's nochmal\" is the recorded retry_cue (at most one per 3.0 s of touch time, unchanged); it waits for an instruction still playing instead of cutting it. NO SYSTEM-VOICE SUBSTITUTE: a study session refuses to start when any of the five recordings is missing from the app, and a missing recording never falls back to the system voice; the device's German system voice no longer matters to a study session, so a missing or changed system voice no longer refuses it. EXPORT: voiceId no longer holds a device voice identifier; it holds the recorded set, \"rec:kiki:\" followed by the first 12 hex characters of the SHA-256 over the five files in the order observe, guided, free-writing, retry, celebration (phase rows and enrolment records). Rows of r8 and earlier carry the device voice identifier or nothing. The five files are pinned by Resources/Prompts/MANIFEST.json and checked in CI."),
        Revision(revision: 10, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-19 06:07", pullRequest: 68,
                 childFacingChange: "Phoneme arm, wherever the letter's own recording plays (observe, guided, free-writing, cold probes): the five phoneme recordings (Letters/{A,F,I,L,M}/<L>_phoneme1.wav) are now mono, 44.1 kHz, 16-bit PCM, the format of the spatial arm's carrier; under r1-r9 they were 2-channel, 48 kHz, 24-bit. Same recordings, measured offline against the r9 files: durations unchanged to the millisecond (A 1.965 s, F 2.000 s, I 1.968 s, L 1.970 s, M 1.970 s); the r9 files were dual-mono (left and right differ by at most 2.7e-4 of full scale), so the mono file is the same signal; steady-state RMS unchanged within 0.01 dB, so the loudness gain the engine applies per file is unchanged; peak level within 0.06 dB except F, +0.94 dB (resampling). The silent and spatial arms are unchanged."),
        Revision(revision: 11, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-21 12:20", pullRequest: 78,
                 childFacingChange: "RECORD-ONLY: nothing a child sees or hears changes, in any arm. EXPORT, phase rows, appended after deviceModel, newest-last, filled on OBSERVE rows only (empty on every other row and on rows of r10 and earlier): observeLossesDuringSound and observeLossesWithoutSound count the audio outputs LOST in that observe phase (the route change iOS reports as the old device unavailable) while the arm's dot-tracked observe sound was playing, so that the rest of it was cut and not replayed, and while no observe sound was playing (the instruction cue, the gap before the presentation, a held observe, after the sound was already cut; in an arm with no observe sound, the whole phase); observeSoundSeconds is how long the observe sound ran, from its start to its stop (the phase's end, a load, an interruption), whatever the output did; observeSoundDeliveredSeconds is the part of it before a lost output, so delivered / ran is the share of the observe sound the child could hear. A loss during the cue leaves the sound to start later on the output then present and counts as without sound. The semantics are the same in every arm: the silent arm has no observe sound, so its observe rows carry observeLossesDuringSound 0, observeSoundSeconds 0.000 and observeSoundDeliveredSeconds 0.000, and every loss there counts as without sound; so does a sound arm's observe whose sound was not claimed (Einmal pro Kondition, the spatial axis-sweep switch). Whether a cut observe sound should be replayed is not decided here."),
        Revision(revision: 12, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-21 18:33", pullRequest: 82,
                 childFacingChange: "Cold probes (pretest, post-test, delayed) are SOUND-OFF in every arm: in the phoneme and spatial arms the pen no longer drives the arm's sound (no playback, no rate, pan or pitch coupling) while the child writes a probe; under r2-r11 a probe's writing sounded like the session's free-writing. The session's own free-writing keeps the arm's sound, ungated as in r6; guided, observe and the silent arm are unchanged. The five recorded spoken prompts are unchanged and identical in every arm. A probe is still refused when the current arm's recording for that letter is missing (r4), although the probe no longer plays it. POST-TEST FOR ALL FIVE LETTERS (David, 2026-10-21, \"Cold probe for all\"): the post-test probe accepts every study letter, so the trained letters get a cold, sound-off post-test after their last session like the untrained ones; under r1-r11 it accepted the two untrained letters only, and a trained letter's post-test was taken to be the free-writing of its final training pass. That free-writing is now practice (with the arm's sound), not the post-test; every outcome measure (pretest, post-test, delayed) is sound-off. Under the all-five comparison switch the post-test is no longer refused. EXPORT: from r12 the export no longer derives a post-test tag for the final training free-writing; rows recorded under r1-r11 keep it. Ruling 2b (David, 2026-10-21): a cut observe sound is recorded only (r11's columns) and never replayed."),
        Revision(revision: 13, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-21 19:59", pullRequest: 83,
                 childFacingChange: "NO SOUND EFFECTS IN ANY ARM (David, 2026-10-21): the stroke-completion tick and the end-of-set success chime, heard in the phoneme and spatial arms only under r4-r12, are removed from every arm, so the three arms differ only in the arm's own sound. Both were supplementary auditory feedback (a knowledge-of-results tick and a reward chime) that two arms received and the silent arm did not, a confound on the sound-versus-silence contrast; r4 had equalised the spoken prompts for the same reason. The five recorded spoken prompts are unchanged and identical in every arm. Haptics are unchanged. The tap chimes belong to the direct phase, which study sessions do not run, so nothing else changes for a child. Non-study behaviour is unchanged."),
        Revision(revision: 14, mergedAtUTC: "2026-10-22 10:36", pullRequest: 84,
                 childFacingChange: "HEADPHONES REQUIRED IN THE SOUND ARMS (David, 2026-10-22): in the phoneme and spatial arms a study session runs only while a headphone-class output (wired, Bluetooth, USB) is connected; without one the session screen is replaced by the proctor-facing \"Keine Kopfhörer verbunden\" stop, live with the route, so unplugging mid-letter stops the letter and plugging back in resumes it where it was (an observe sound cut by the unplug is not replayed; r11 records it). Under r1-r13 the session ran on the loudspeaker with only a research-dashboard banner. The silent arm and the panning-off comparison configuration are unaffected. Automated tests waive the requirement with a launch argument no on-device control can set; the output route on every row shows such a run. ENROLMENT LEAVES THE FIRST TRAINED LETTER PARKED (audit R-007, 2026-10-22): under r1-r13 in-place enrolment started that letter's observe at once, so its demonstration and arm sound played before its own cold pretest; now nothing plays until the proctor opens a pretest or starts the letter, as after a fresh launch."),
        Revision(revision: 15, mergedAtUTC: "2026-11-01 20:56", pullRequest: 89,
                 childFacingChange: "RECORD-ONLY: nothing a child sees or hears changes, in any arm. THE OUTGOING CHILD'S IN-FLIGHT LETTER IS RECORDED UNDER THE OUTGOING ID BEFORE THE SEAL (audit R-015, 2026-10-13): \"Nächstes Kind\" and \"Teilnehmer wiederherstellen\" now record the letter on screen exactly as a letter change does, before the outgoing child is sealed and before the identity switches: a pen still down is ended, a finished but not yet scored free-writing (the 2.0 s quiet window pending, or the recognizer in flight) is scored and recorded, and a letter left mid-phase gets its completed false row. Under r1-r14 the switch discarded that trial, so an end-of-session abandonment never got its completed false row (completion rates biased up), a finished but unscored free-writing was lost, and a child who stopped inside their first letter had no row, was not sealed, and survived only as an enrolment record. Nothing of the outgoing child is recorded under the incoming id, and the incoming child inherits none of it."),
        Revision(revision: 16, mergedAtUTC: "2026-11-02 13:17", pullRequest: 90,
                 childFacingChange: "Bug-hunt rounds 2026-11-01/02, David's rulings 2026-11-02. HEADPHONES IN EVERY ARM (B6): the r14 headphone requirement now applies to the silent arm too, so the spoken prompts reach every arm through the same transducer; the proctor banner shows in every arm. UNPLUG = INTERRUPTION (B1): losing the headphones mid-letter freezes the session like any interruption (no arm sound, no prompt, no stroke, the letter does not run on behind the stop screen) and re-plugging restarts the letter from its instruction cue (D9 default); under r14 the observe demonstration completed behind the stop and \"Jetzt du.\" played. While a session is blocked or suspended no arm sound, prompt or stroke starts (r14 intent, now enforced). SOUND, sound arms: a faded stop no longer ends in a ~90 ms full-level burst (C1); an interruption that begins during a stop fade no longer brings the sound back looping (C2); a lifecycle blip with no file loaded no longer pauses the engine 0.2 s later (C3); after the last lift the ~1.4 s tail plays at the pre-flick smoothed rate, no longer at the flick rate up to 2.0x (B17); spatial pitch is written at most once per display frame (B18; measured onset unchanged, spatial still ~30 ms after phoneme, recorded as a constant confound). GUIDED TRACKING (StrokeTracker): every following checkpoint within the radius is consumed per touch sample, so a brisk correct trace completes (B9; under r1-r15 crossbars stopped registering above ~300 pt/s); the hit radius scales with glyph height only and the hit test is isotropic, so every letter gets the same tolerance in points, horizontally and vertically (B8; under r1-r15 it scaled with glyph area, I 8.9 pt vs M 21.8 pt, and vertical error weighed 1.73x); a straight line through a narrow letter's checkpoints in order counts (B7, ruled: no path-length gate). Guided durations are therefore not comparable with r1-r15 rows. A second contact elsewhere on the screen (a thumb on the rail) no longer makes the canvas ignore the trace (A20), and when a palm is down first, tracking hands over to the finger that moves (B10; the study build drops the two-finger swipe, which held back the first moves). A trial in flight when a relaunch is pending is recorded as completed false (A3). NAVIGATION: a relaunch and the chevron after a probe go to the first unfinished trained letter (A9; under r1-r15 a relaunch reopened letter 1 and one tap retrained it under the same id); study chevrons no longer wrap at either end (A10); with letter repeats above 1 the end-of-set celebration fires once, after the last pass (A11). PARTICIPANTS: the outgoing child's prompt and queued retry stop at \"Nächstes Kind\" and at a restore (A4); an arm or subset override set after a child has rows is refused and the recorded assignment kept, and the pickers lock (A7, A24); a restore without its enrolment record takes the arm from the child's archived rows, and refuses when they disagree (A5); probe attempts count the child's archived sessions (A6); no cold probe without an enrolled child (A8); an outgoing child with no activity at all (no touch, probe, unpark, row or duration) has its enrolment record DROPPED (B2); the Research Dashboard shows a per-arm and per-subset child tally and the confirm names a pending override (B5). SCORING (free-writing and cold probes): normalisation is isotropic, by canvas height (B8); the bounding-box fit uses only the strokes matched to the letter (match, fit, re-match), and an axis sets the fit scale only if the letter spans at least a quarter of its larger extent on it, so a stray dot or a narrow letter's jitter no longer moves the r6 primary outcome (B11); each stroke is resampled to a fixed arc-length spacing before Frechet (B15); tempoConsistency ignores pen lifts and measures the variance of within-stroke speed in 0.1 s windows (B13); the recogniser's calibrated confidence is a softmax probability, the raw column stays the raw model score (B16); topThree is sorted by calibrated confidence (A17). The guided phase score of a completed row is always 1.0 (B-16, documented, unchanged). EXPORT: rows carry the enrolment id and a child's rows are selected by it, with a droppedPreEnrolmentRows count (B3); an archive file that could not be loaded is named in a setAside line (B4); the export screen first records a finished but held free-writing (finalizedBy export, B22); timestamps carry fractional seconds, NaN is written NaN, the combined CSV ends with a newline, undefined averages are empty or null, the date column is Gregorian (A14-A16). VISUAL: child ink #059669 and guide #D97706 (were #10B981 / #F59E0B; contrast on the canvas paper 3.60:1 and 3.04:1, B20); regular and medium text uses the bundled Primae / PrimaeText faces instead of falling back to the system font (A25); a single app scene (A26). PROMPTS: .phaseDirect is no longer a study spoken key (A29); the five recordings are unchanged."),
        Revision(revision: 17, mergedAtUTC: nil, pullRequest: nil,
                 childFacingChange: "LIVE SONIFICATION ENGINE (RULINGS L1-L4, 2026-11-02/03), sound arms, every phase that sounds (observe demonstration, guided, free-writing): the arm sound is rendered by a grain engine of our own in a node beside AudioEngine instead of AVAudioPlayerNode -> AVAudioUnitTimePitch. The sound now STARTS WITH THE STROKE: measured first pen move -> audible on the study iPad (iPad13,8, iOS 27.2, loudspeaker route, Debug-Study, 2026-11-03; -20 dB re steady at the mixer, plus the session's outputLatency 5.8 ms): median 13.3-15.8 ms, max 18.3 ms, N=8 strokes per arm, both arms (mixer median 7.5-10 ms; LiveSonificationOnsetDeviceTests; headphone routes unmeasured) (r16: 230 ms phoneme / 268 ms spatial, LAT.md), so the first ~35-40 pt of every stroke are no longer silent, and the r16 spatial-after-phoneme onset confound (+35 ms) is gone. Rate and pitch FOLLOW THE PEN FASTER (TDPSOLA, smoothing tau 2 ms: rate step 1->2 within 2.5 ms of ideal, pinned; pitch step ~9 ms to 90 %, lane OPT-T bench; r16 ~100 ms and ~68 ms). The phoneme still starts at its attack on every gate-open, and its pitch is held at every rate (L1); the spatial carrier is the same recording, transposed per grain (L2). The I/O buffer is 5 ms (was 21.3 ms), session-wide. SOUND ENVELOPE BY STROKE (RULINGS 2026-11-05, every sound arm and phase that sounds identically): the sound starts at once when the pen starts a stroke and fades at once (the standing 0.4 s stop fade, no hold) when the letter's last stroke reaches its end or the pen lifts with the letter complete (free-writing included: the fade starts when the tracker sees the last stroke end and the sound stays off until the next touch). BETWEEN STROKES the hold that keeps the sound going across a lift ADAPTS to the child: hold = clamp(2.0 x running median of the child's last 5 lift-to-next-touch gaps, 0.5 s, 2.5 s), seeded from the fixed 0.8 s of r5-r16 (two seed gaps of 0.4 s, so a child's first strokes hear 0.8 s); gaps over 3 s are breaks and are not measured; cold probes neither feed nor read it; it restarts from the seed for every enrolled child. The stall cutoff (a pen that stops while touching) scales with the same tempo: 0.3 s x hold/0.8 s, between 0.3 s and 0.6 s. A stroke that starts inside the hold continues the sound (never replays its attack); after a real stop it starts at its attack. A pitch jump over 300 cents while the sound is held (spatial arm: F's second stroke starts at the top of the pitch range where the stem ended at the bottom) glides over 40 ms instead of 2 ms; in-stroke pitch tracking is unchanged. EXPORT: phase rows append liftHoldSeconds, stallSeconds (in force at the end of the phase), strokeGaps and strokeGapMedianSeconds (measured in the phase) after soundEngine, in every arm. Unchanged: the 0.4 s stop fade, pan, the AE-2 loudness target (the files, the coupling map and where AE-2 measures a phoneme changed in R17D, below). ENGINE: TDPSOLA (David 2026-11-03, after an in-app blind A/B against a second engine, which is not shipped). NO CUTS AND CLICKS IN THE TRANSITIONS (lane LOOPX, 2026-11-03): a stroke that starts while the previous sound is still fading out (after a 0.3 s stall or after the 0.8 s lift hold) used to cut the sound for ~5 ms before the phoneme restarted at its attack; it now restarts at its attack crossing over from the fading sound (equal power, 20 ms); a switch to another sound while one is held fades it in 20 ms instead of 3 ms. The pan is unchanged (the mixer already slews it, measured). EXPORT: phase rows append soundEngine (\"TDPSOLA\") after observeSoundDeliveredSeconds. PHONEME ONSETS + NEW F LOOP (RULINGS 2026-11-05 evening/night and 2026-11-06, lane R17D), phoneme arm, every phase that sounds: each of the five phoneme files now opens with the recording's own onset, cut from David's lossless takes, before the loop: A 422 ms (rough glottal start, then the loud vowel start), I 96 ms (breathy start), L 132 ms and M 153 ms (voiced rise